+
+## 20. Non-Negotiable Design Principles
+
+### 20.1 No One-State Market Model
+
+The market cannot be treated as always reverting or always trending.
+
+```text
+Every setup must know whether it is operating in Hook, Rally, Transition, or Unknown state.
+```
+
+### 20.2 No Raw Extreme Trading
+
+An extreme is not enough.
+
+```text
+Extreme + phase + X-zone + fractal alignment + optionality = candidate trade.
+```
+
+### 20.3 No Mechanical Node Reversal in Rally
+
+Raw node-counting against an active Rally is dangerous.
+
+```text
+In Rally phase, F-counting must dominate node-counting.
+```
+
+### 20.4 No Candle Confirmation Dependency
+
+Candle confirmation can be recorded, but the primary edge should not depend on late confirmation if the goal is convex entry.
+
+### 20.5 X and Y Must Work Together
+
+```text
+X gives level.
+Y gives permission.
+```
+
+Neither alone is sufficient.
+
+### 20.6 Optionality Must Be Earned
+
+A large target does not create optionality.
+
+```text
+Optionality must be supported by structure, state, invalidation, and path.
+```
+
+---
+
+## 21. Final Research Statement
+
+The Flag Project favors reverse extreme entries because they can create superior optionality when selected correctly.
+
+The system should not reverse every move. It should classify the market into Hook and Rally phases, understand the fractal relationship between timeframes, use node-counting primarily in Hook conditions, use F-counting primarily in Rally conditions, and execute only where the X-axis provides a precise structural extreme while the Y-axis grants state permission.
+
+The best trades may occur when a lower-timeframe movement reaches a late F-state into a higher-timeframe X-zone, allowing a limit entry near invalidation while targeting the higher-timeframe destination.
+
+In this model:
+
+```text
+Hook phase teaches where liquidity reacts.
+Rally phase teaches where energy expands.
+X-axis gives the exact battlefield.
+Y-axis gives the permission to fight.
+Fractal alignment determines whether a lower-timeframe reversal is actually a higher-timeframe continuation.
+Optionality determines whether the trade is worth taking.
+```
+
+This document should guide future Flag engineering, setup design, validation metrics, and execution research.
+
